WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.385
Shoppers look around, pick things up, and

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:03.560
then they're gone.

00:00:03.635 --> 00:00:05.585
Most shops never find out why.

00:00:06.585 --> 00:00:07.710
Meet Leo.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.385
He runs a small furniture shop in

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:10.560
a busy city.

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:13.025
Every day, shoppers walk by, browse a

00:00:13.075 --> 00:00:14.645
little, and leave.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.895
Leo has clues: what they looked at,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.545
what they almost bought, and how they

00:00:18.595 --> 00:00:19.520
paid.

00:00:19.595 --> 00:00:22.145
But those clues live in different places,

00:00:22.195 --> 00:00:24.545
so every campaign is a guess.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:25.795
Who should he reach?

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.470
And what should he offer?

00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:29.843
So, could AI help Leo decide?

00:00:30.443 --> 00:00:33.246
Meet Market Metro: a living, simulated city

00:00:33.246 --> 00:00:36.277
where AI agents observe shoppers, train models

00:00:36.277 --> 00:00:38.702
and test marketing campaigns.

00:00:38.702 --> 00:00:41.960
Then we measure what changed: purchases, merchant

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.218
contribution and PayPal payment outcomes.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.790
First, the data needs meaning.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:51.150
Our ontology connects shoppers, products, contacts and

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.880
payments, so every prediction learns from the

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.280
right examples.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.710
Recommendations and inquiries never get mixed up.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.090
And one strict rule: earlier signals can

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.330
be inputs, but later outcomes are kept

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.360
for labels and evaluation.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.125
Now the planner agent gets to work.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.000
It studies demand and available stock, picks

00:01:11.075 --> 00:01:13.240
twenty eight days of history, and asks

00:01:13.315 --> 00:01:16.465
local tools to train two candidate models.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.540
Every decision, tool call and result becomes

00:01:19.565 --> 00:01:21.415
a checkpoint you can open.

00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:23.793
But nothing ships on a hunch.

00:01:23.793 --> 00:01:26.404
The verifier tests both models in matching

00:01:26.404 --> 00:01:30.175
development markets, checking PayPal and merchant outcomes

00:01:30.175 --> 00:01:31.408
side by side.

00:01:31.408 --> 00:01:34.236
Only a tested winner is published; otherwise,

00:01:34.236 --> 00:01:36.267
the current policy stays.

00:01:36.617 --> 00:01:38.067
Day thirty five.

00:01:38.142 --> 00:01:41.217
A recommendation reaches our quiet shopper, sixteen

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.392
days after their last purchase.

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.317
They buy a ninety six dollar side

00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:47.897
table, and it arrives the next day.

00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:50.372
Under fixed rules, that same request went

00:01:50.447 --> 00:01:51.447
unfilled.

00:01:51.683 --> 00:01:53.726
And the money leaves a trail.

00:01:53.726 --> 00:01:56.586
PayPal Sandbox captures that exact order: ninety

00:01:56.586 --> 00:01:58.833
six dollars gross, the provider fee and

00:01:58.833 --> 00:02:00.740
the merchant's proceeds.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.988
Each record links back to the warehouse

00:02:02.988 --> 00:02:05.303
order, so the proof is traceable without

00:02:05.303 --> 00:02:06.733
double counting.

00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:08.601
So, what changed?

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:11.427
Across the same market, sixteen more purchases

00:02:11.427 --> 00:02:13.334
were completed and kept.

00:02:13.334 --> 00:02:15.878
Payment volume grows by two thousand three

00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:17.997
hundred forty five dollars.

00:02:17.997 --> 00:02:21.035
Merchant contribution and modeled PayPal net both

00:02:21.035 --> 00:02:22.943
rise, even after AI cost.

00:02:22.943 --> 00:02:25.416
Every number is measured against the same

00:02:25.416 --> 00:02:26.617
fixed rules.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.320
Don't take our word for it.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.687
AG Grid lets judges inspect the entire

00:02:31.687 --> 00:02:35.605
journey: trained models, agent decisions, campaign outcomes

00:02:35.605 --> 00:02:37.972
and the native PayPal receipt.

00:02:37.972 --> 00:02:41.225
Open any checkpoint and follow the evidence.

00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:43.858
For merchants like Leo, those two questions

00:02:43.908 --> 00:02:45.808
now have tested answers.

00:02:45.908 --> 00:02:47.108
Market Metro.

00:02:47.183 --> 00:02:50.158
Agentic commerce that learns, tests its ideas,

00:02:50.218 --> 00:02:51.543
and shows its work.
